<h1>Isometric grid</h1>
<p>Isometric games place objects on a diamond-shaped grid rather than a flat square one. This page explains what that placement means, how the grid converts tiles to world positions, and how to check, preview, and path across it.</p>

<h2>What isometric placement is</h2>
<p>In a top-down square grid, one tile is one square of floor. In an <strong>isometric</strong> view, the same logical tile is drawn as a diamond: the camera looks at the world from an angle, so “north-east” on the ground runs up-right on screen. Characters, crates, and walls still occupy integer tile cells — only the way cells map to world and screen positions changes.</p>
<p>Isometric placement exists so a Skyforge station deck can read as 2.5D: sprites sort by depth, props line up on the diamond lattice, and gameplay still reasons in simple tile coordinates.</p>
<p>Two components cooperate:</p>
<ul>
  <li><code>IsoTransform</code> places one entity on the diamond lattice and handles depth sorting for its <a href="{{ path('docs_page', {slug: 'sprites-and-animation'}) }}">sprite</a>.</li>
  <li><code>IsoGrid</code> defines the lattice itself: tile size, origin, and which cells are occupied.</li>
</ul>

<h2>IsoTransform versus Transform2D</h2>
<p>Ordinary <code>Transform2D</code> places an entity at a free world position <code>[x, y]</code> with any rotation and scale. That is right for floating HUD-adjacent props and parallax art.</p>
<p><code>IsoTransform</code> places an entity at a tile and derives its world position from the grid. It is right for anything that should sit on the lattice: units, buildings, pickups on the deck.</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th></th><th><code>Transform2D</code></th><th><code>IsoTransform</code></th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Position means</td><td>World units</td><td>Tile coordinates</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Depth sorting</td><td>By <code>sorting_order</code> and layer</td><td>Also by isometric depth, so upper tiles draw behind lower ones</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Use for</td><td>Free placement, cameras, effects</td><td>Anything standing on the grid</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>Both still interact with the same sprite draw order: <code>sorting_order</code>, then layer name, then isometric depth.</p>

<h2>Grid configuration</h2>
<pre><code>{
  "IsoGrid": {
    "tile_width": 64,
    "tile_height": 32,
    "origin": [0, 0],
    "size": [40, 40]
  }
}</code></pre>
<ul>
  <li><code>tile_width</code> is the full diamond width in world units.</li>
  <li><code>tile_height</code> is the full diamond height in world units. The 2:1 ratio is the classic isometric diamond.</li>
  <li><code>origin</code> is the world position of tile <code>(0, 0)</code>.</li>
  <li><code>size</code> is <code>[columns, rows]</code> of cells the grid owns.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Tile and world conversion</h2>
<p>Gameplay thinks in tiles; rendering and physics think in world units. The <code>demi.grid</code> module converts between them.</p>
<pre><code>local Grid = require("demi.grid")

print(Grid.available(grid))                    -- boolean

local wx, wy = Grid.tile_to_world(grid, tx, ty)
local tx, ty = Grid.world_to_tile(grid, wx, wy)

local tile = Grid.get_tile(grid, tx, ty)
local occupant = Grid.entity_at(grid, tx, ty)</code></pre>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Call</th><th>Arguments</th><th>Returns</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>available</code></td><td>grid id</td><td><code>true</code> when the grid is configured and ready, otherwise <code>false</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>tile_to_world</code></td><td>grid id, tile <code>tx</code>, <code>ty</code></td><td>Two scalars <code>wx, wy</code> — the world position of that tile’s center on the diamond.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>world_to_tile</code></td><td>grid id, world <code>wx</code>, <code>wy</code></td><td>Two scalars <code>tx, ty</code> — the tile under that world point.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>get_tile</code></td><td>grid id, <code>tx</code>, <code>ty</code></td><td>Tile ID number at the cell, or <code>0</code> when empty.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>entity_at</code></td><td>grid id, <code>tx</code>, <code>ty</code></td><td>Entity handle occupying the cell, or <code>nil</code> when the cell is free.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>Placing a crate the player just bought in Skyforge’s market:</p>
<pre><code>local Grid = require("demi.grid")

local wx, wy = Grid.tile_to_world(grid, 12, 7)
-- wx, wy → world position of tile (12, 7)

local tx, ty = Grid.world_to_tile(grid, wx, wy)
-- tx, ty → 12, 7 (round-trips back to the same cell)</code></pre>
<ul>
  <li><code>tile_to_world</code> returns two scalars suitable for a <code>Transform2D</code> position.</li>
  <li><code>world_to_tile</code> returns two scalars in tile units, truncated to the cell that contains the point.</li>
  <li>Use <code>tile_to_world</code> to spawn; use <code>world_to_tile</code> to interpret mouse clicks.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Placement checks and previews</h2>
<p>Building games need to answer “may I build here?” before committing, and show a ghost while the player aims.</p>
<pre><code>local Grid = require("demi.grid")

if Grid.can_place(grid, tx, ty) then
    Grid.set_preview(grid, tx, ty, 17)
else
    Grid.clear_preview(grid)
end</code></pre>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Call</th><th>Arguments</th><th>Returns</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>can_place</code></td><td>grid id, <code>tx</code>, <code>ty</code></td><td><code>true</code> when the cell accepts a new occupant (in bounds, not blocked, unoccupied); otherwise <code>false</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>set_preview</code></td><td>grid id, <code>tx</code>, <code>ty</code>, tile ID to ghost</td><td>Nothing. Draws a translucent preview at the cell.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>clear_preview</code></td><td>grid id</td><td>Nothing. Removes the ghost immediately.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>Clear the preview whenever the player cancels placement or the cursor leaves the grid so no ghost is left behind.</p>

<h2>Grid paths</h2>
<p>Units walking across the deck need routes around walls and other units.</p>
<pre><code>local Grid = require("demi.grid")

local path, diagnostic = Grid.path(grid, from, to)
-- path       array of { x, y } world points, or nil on failure
-- diagnostic table explaining why a path could not be built</code></pre>
<ul>
  <li><code>from</code> and <code>to</code> are tile or world endpoints accepted by the grid’s path request.</li>
  <li><code>path</code> is an array of <code>{ x, y }</code> world points to walk in order, or <code>nil</code> when no route exists.</li>
  <li><code>diagnostic</code> is a table describing the failure (blocked start, unreachable goal). Successful calls may return no diagnostic.</li>
</ul>
<p>Guard with <code>if path then</code> before iterating — a blocked corridor is a <code>nil</code> path, not an empty array.</p>

<h2>Independent navigation grids</h2>
<p><code>demi.navigation</code> creates independently owned logical grids. Each grid stores its own blockers, traversal costs and dimensions. Use the world default grid when you want to share Tilemap navigation and debug visualization; create another grid for a separate map or terrain surface.</p>
<pre><code>local Navigation = require("demi.navigation")
local nav = assert(Navigation.create_grid(32, 32, 2, 0, 0))
-- Alternatively: local nav = Navigation.default_grid()
nav:set_blocked(3, 4, true)
nav:set_cost(5, 5, 2)
local path, diagnostic = nav:path(0, 0, 8, 8, true)
for _, point in ipairs(path) do
    print(point[1], point[2], point.world_x, point.world_y)
end</code></pre>
<table><thead><tr><th>Call</th><th>Behavior</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><code>Navigation.create_grid(width, height, cell_size, origin_x?, origin_y?)</code></td><td>Returns a new grid, or nil for invalid dimensions, scale or origin. Origins default to zero.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>Navigation.default_grid()</code></td><td>Returns the world grid shared with Tilemap navigation and debug display.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>nav:configure(width, height, cell_size, origin_x?, origin_y?)</code></td><td>Reconfigures this grid and clears its blockers/costs; returns success.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>nav:set_blocked(column, row, blocked)</code> / <code>nav:blocked(column, row)</code></td><td>Sets or reads a blocked cell. Coordinates are zero-based; out-of-bounds cells are blocked.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>nav:set_cost(column, row, cost)</code></td><td>Sets a finite traversal cost of at least 1; returns success.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>nav:path(start_col, start_row, goal_col, goal_row, diagonal?)</code></td><td>Returns an array including start and goal, or an empty array on failure, plus a diagnostic string. Success is <code>OK</code>. Diagonals default to false and do not cut blocked corners.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>nav:world_to_cell(x, y)</code> / <code>nav:cell_to_world(column, row)</code></td><td>Returns two coordinates, or nil,nil outside the grid. World coordinates describe cell centres.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>nav:clear()</code> / <code>nav:available()</code></td><td>Clear disables the grid until configured again; available reports whether it is configured.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>Path points contain numeric column/row entries <code>[1]</code>, <code>[2]</code> plus <code>world_x</code> and <code>world_y</code> fields. These are logical planar coordinates: a ground navigator can map the second axis to world Z and obtain height separately.</p>
<h2>Ground agents in lightweight 3D</h2>
<p>The <code>demi.navigation.ground</code> package adds incremental terrain surveys and agents driven by <code>CharacterController3D</code>. It uses independent native grids, reports blocked/unreachable routes, pauses disabled agents and replans when footprint obstacles change. Gameplay chooses jobs and priorities. This supports heightfield ground movement; it is not a multilevel navmesh or crowd-avoidance system.</p>
<pre><code>local Ground = require("demi.navigation.ground")
local surface = Ground.create({terrain="terrain", columns=64, rows=64,
    cell_size=2, origin={0,0}, ignored_entity="engineer"})
local agent = surface:agent("engineer", {speed=3})
-- Update the survey until ready, then issue a destination once.
surface:update(64)
if surface:ready() then agent:move_to({30,0,40}, 3) end
-- In fixed update: agent:update(dt)</code></pre>
<p>The actor needs a transform, collider and CharacterController3D. See the package README for lifecycle, resurvey and obstacle updates.</p>
<h2>Development migration</h2>
<p>The old <code>demi.navigation2d</code> service is replaced by <code>demi.navigation</code>. Use <code>Navigation.default_grid()</code> for the former world grid and change calls to colon methods, or create independently owned grids. There is no legacy alias. Opening a script regenerates the editor’s native LuaLS annotations and removes obsolete native module stubs; custom annotation files remain. Native grid algorithms and Tilemap integration remain shared.</p>

<h2>Choosing tools</h2>
<ul>
  <li>One unit moving A to B on an open deck: <code>Grid.path</code>.</li>
  <li>Fleets routing around dynamic hazards with costs: <code>demi.navigation</code>.</li>
  <li>Build-mode ghosts: <code>can_place</code> + <code>set_preview</code>.</li>
  <li>Mouse picking into tile coordinates: <code>world_to_tile</code>.</li>
  <li>Depth-correct sprite stacking: <code>IsoTransform</code>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Level content itself comes from <a href="{{ path('docs_page', {slug: 'tilemaps'}) }}">Tilemaps</a>, and the art drawn on each cell is standard <a href="{{ path('docs_page', {slug: 'sprites-and-animation'}) }}">sprite</a> work.</p>